         reading : the budget is stated in both forms; j is the comparable number.

====================================================================================================
PART 2 -- THE INFALL SOURCE: G182's stream vs the core's angular momentum
====================================================================================================
  the coherent stream crossing at impact parameter b carries j_infall = b v_ff:
      at b = 1 R500 (0.70 Mpc): j_infall = 1.700e+05 kpc.km/s
      at b = 1 R500 with the phase-mixed caustic speed instead: j = 9.816e+04 kpc.km/s
  vs the beta-implied core budget j_core = 2.239e+05 kpc.km/s:
      impact parameter that closes the budget: b_req = j_core/v_ff = 0.922 Mpc = 1.32 R500
      budget covered at b = 1 R500: j_infall/j_core = 76% (full coverage needs b = 1.32 R500)
  THE PHASE-MIXING TENSION: the ordered component needed v_rot = 376 km/s = 2.68 x the caustic dispersion sigma_d = 140.2 km/s
      (the G182 caustic sheet is phase-mixed to sigma_d; a coherent crossing stream keeps a bulk 
       component ~ v_ff, so the ordered v_tan is not excluded by the caustic reading -- registered)
  [PASS] C4 [part 2] j_infall vs the beta-implied core j: the geometry that closes the budget
         measured: b_req = 0.922 Mpc = 1.32 R500; f(b=1R500) = 1.32
         reading : the infall CAN carry the beta-implied specific angular momentum to order unity: a coherent stream crossing at ~1.3 R500 (a plausible filament impact parameter) transfers 100% -- amplitude-consistent geometry.
  [PASS] C5 [part 2] the phase-mixing tension registered
         measured: v_rot,req/sigma_d = 2.68
         reading : the ordered component is 2.7x the phase-mixed caustic dispersion: for the transfer to deposit coherent spin the stream must remain largely un-phase-mixed down to the core -- a registered physical requirement, not a contradiction.

====================================================================================================
PART 3 -- THE SIGNATURE TEST: the projected v_los gradient at the core bins
         (the rotation amplitude and axis on the sky, from the committed v_los)
====================================================================================================
  per-cluster gradient fit (weighted linear v_los = v0 + a X + b Y, X/Y in that cluster's R500):
      clusters fitted: 58 (all with >=10 core members); median n = 46
      mean gradient amplitude <A> = 313.0 +- 25.6 km/s per R500
      shuffle-null <A_null> = 282.6 (per-cluster null spread 78.1)
  THE EXCESS (the signal): <A> - <A_null> = +30.5 +- 23.6 km/s per R500  ->  z = +1.29
      median excess +7.0; coherent vector mean +51.2, -26.8 km/s/R500 (amp 57.8, z = +1.71 vs 0; null-vector amplitude 35.6 -> z = +0.66)
      axis distribution: Rayleigh Rbar = 0.073, z = +0.79 (uniform if small)
      individual rotators: 5/58 clusters with A_k > 3 sigma_k
  THE ROTATION AMPLITUDE: v_rot,los = excess x <R> = 25.9 +- 20.0 km/s (2-sigma UL 65.9 km/s)
  VS THE PREDICTION (beta-implied): A_pred = v_rot <sin i>/<R> = 348 km/s per R500, v_rot,los,pred = v_rot <sin i> = 296 km/s
      -> the measured gradient sits 13.5 sigma BELOW the beta-implied rotation prediction
