  robustness: unweighted fit (no cz-error weights) gives the same conclusion by construction of the noise test; the per-cluster bootstrap errors (median 187 km/s per R500) confirm the noise floor near the null spread.

====================================================================================================
PART 4 -- THE VERDICTS
====================================================================================================
  V1 THE IMPLIED ANGULAR MOMENTUM.  From the committed beta_inner = -0.099 +- 0.074:
     (a) the anisotropy reading: sigma_t/sigma_r = 1.049 +- 0.035 -- a 4.8% tangential per-axis dispersion excess, +1.38 sigma from isotropic;
     (b) the ordered-rotation reading: v_rot = 376 +- 139 km/s at the core's sigma_r = 844 km/s, i.e. specific j_core = r v_tan = 2.24e+05 kpc.km/s (6.9e+27 m^2/s per unit mass); the tracer-weighted budget L = N m r v_tan = 6.36e-01 kg m^2/s over the 10,145-catalog (N = 3146 in the [0.2,1.5] shell: 1.97e-01 kg m^2/s).  THE BUDGET'S CONTENT: the comparable number is the SPECIFIC angular momentum j_core = 2.2e+05 kpc.km/s; the SI L is small because m = 5.09 keV is a particle-scale mass.
  V2 THE INFALL-TRANSFER BALANCE.  THE INFALL CAN SUPPLY THE BETA-IMPLIED SPIN TO ORDER UNITY: a coherent G182 stream (v_ff = 242.9 km/s) crossing at b_req = 0.92 Mpc = 1.32 R500 carries exactly j_core (a 1-R500 crossing covers 76% of the budget; full at b = 1.32 R500) -- the crossing geometry is amplitude-consistent.  BUT THE DIRECT MEASUREMENT DECIDES: the committed v_los field gives the core's rotation gradient excess +30 +- 24 km/s per R500 (z = +1.3), so the MEASURED core j = 1.5e+04 +- 1.2e+04 kpc.km/s (2-sigma UL 3.9e+04) vs j_infall(b = 1 R500) = 1.7e+05 kpc.km/s: the transferred fraction is f = 0.09 measured, UL 0.23 (<= 23%) -- the infall geometry EXPLAINS the twist in amplitude only if a twist existed; the measured twist is ~0, so the balance closes as 'stream available, spin not deposited' (or the coherent component phase-mixes before the core: v_rot,req = 2.7 x sigma_d).
  V3 THE HONEST STATEMENT -- THE NUMBER THAT SAYS WHETHER THE DARK CORE ROTATES:  the projected v_los gradient at the core bins [0.2, 1.5] R500 (3,146 HeCS members, 58 clusters, the committed v_los) is +30.5 +- 23.6 km/s per R500 over the shuffle-null (z = +1.29), i.e. a projected rotation amplitude v_rot,los = +25.9 +- 20.0 km/s (2-sigma UL 65.9 km/s), axis distribution uniform (Rayleigh z = +0.79), aligned component null-consistent (z = +0.66).  THE PHANTOM CORE DOES NOT ROTATE at the level the beta-implied spin would demand: the beta_inner = -0.10 rotation reading (v_rot = 376 km/s, A_pred = 348 km/s per R500) is excluded at >10 sigma by the direct kinematics.  The negative inner beta is better read as MILD TANGENTIAL DISPERSION ANISOTROPY (sigma_t/sigma_r = 1.05, only 4.8% above radial, itself 1.5 sigma from isotropic), not as ordered rotation; the infall's angular momentum is geometrically sufficient but kinematically undeposited -- the core's spin, measured, is CONSISTENT WITH ZERO.
  [PASS] C9 [verdicts] V1/V2/V3 stated from the computed numbers
         measured: see PART 4

